# THE MAZE. A lattice of 2-unit walls with one way in, one way out, and rewards
# in the dead ends.
#
# WHY THIS IS A LEVEL AND NOT A PUZZLE. The camera is fixed top-down at 45
# degrees, so the whole maze is on screen: nobody is DISCOVERING the route, they
# are choosing one and executing it while the party spreads out. That is the
# opposite of what a maze usually is, and it is the reason this one is braided
# rather than perfect -- a single-solution maze read from above is not a puzzle,
# it is a queue. The loops mean two players can take different routes and still
# arrive, which is the only version of a maze that suits a co-op game where the
# camera frames everyone. Carved at 14 loops over 80 cells; the first version of
# this file managed five over seventy and played close to single file.
#
# THE OUTER LANES ARE THE BRIDGE'S OWN EDGE (2026-08-17). Corridors sit on EVEN
# columns, so x = 0 and x = 14 are open deck against the parapet rather than a
# wall column spent on a boundary the deck already has: `has_wall` railings any
# solid cell in the outermost column, and WALL_HEIGHT is 2.0, exactly the height
# a maze wall is. It buys a lane -- eight instead of seven -- and it stops the
# maze reading as a sealed box, because the outer lanes have the real deck edge
# beside them and the drop past it, like every other section.
#
# That lane was WALKED before this was authored: see test_edge_lane. A parapet is
# a 0.3 m slab inset at the cell edge and this repo has three notes about
# flat-bottomed bodies catching on exactly that kind of boundary.
#
# WALLS ARE TWO UNITS, AND THAT NUMBER IS A SIGHTLINE (not a climb -- there is no
# step-up in this game, so ANY height is impassable and one unit would block just
# as well). At a 45-degree pitch a wall of height h hides exactly h metres of
# ground behind it, and a cell is 2 m: so two units hides the FLOOR of the cell
# beyond, and one unit would hide half of it and read as a kerb. A player is 1.8 m
# though, so heads still clear the wall almost everywhere -- you lose track of
# what is on the ground, never of where your friends are.
#
# THE `X` GLYPH IS A WALL KIND, not raised deck. They build identically -- see
# GridConfig.Kind.WALL -- but a wall is NOT SOLID, which is what stops the
# validator counting every wall top as marooned deck and rejecting the file.
#
# ONE DOOR EACH END. A full-width mouth would let the party fan out before the
# maze had asked them anything; a single opening makes the entrance a place, and
# puts everyone in the same corridor for the first moment.
#
# THE DEAD ENDS THAT SURVIVED THE BRAID HOLD THE REWARDS -- hats and one heart.
# From above you can SEE them, so a detour is a decision about time rather than a
# discovery, which is the only way a dead end earns its place in a maze you can
# see the whole of.
#
# FLOOR TRAPS: three timed floors (%) and three spike blocks (v). A maze read
# from above is never a route problem, so the only thing that can make choosing a
# route cost anything is what is standing on it.
#
# BOTH ARE TIMING GATES, and neither can make a route impossible. A timed floor
# is periodically solid -- gone for 65 ticks of every 180 -- and spikes are out
# for 34% of a 2 s cycle with a ramp either side as a telegraph, which leaves the
# cell harmless 76% of the time and charges one health of five when it does catch
# you. Neither blocks; both cost time, or health, from somebody who walked in
# without reading the rhythm.
#
# THIS FILE SAID THE OPPOSITE FOR ONE AFTERNOON. It claimed a spike could SEAL a
# corridor and that every one had been checked against the maze's connectivity.
# That was a false model of the mechanic, argued from "a maze corridor is one cell
# wide so there is no stepping around it" while never looking at SPIKE_PERIOD. It
# is written down because the mistake is instructive: the reasoning was sound and
# the premise was never measured.
#
# No trap touches another: spikes beside a timed floor is a hazard aimed at
# somebody standing still waiting for the floor to come back.
#
# NOT DRESSED, like every authored segment (BridgeGrid.load_segment_file): worth
# knowing here specifically, because a hazard budget has no idea what a corridor
# is and the reachability flood cannot see content -- a spiked maze would still
# validate as crossable.

name = run_maze
base_height = 0
width = 21
length = 24
tags = maze, walls, navigation

